    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d36-a4">

                <pb facs="0199" n="174" sameAs="frus:frus1977-80v04/pg174"/>


                <head>Paper Prepared in the Department of Defense<anchor n="17" xml:id="d36-f1"
                    /></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian">undated</date></dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#italic">SURVIVABILITY OF US COMMAND AND
                        CONTROL SYSTEMS</hi></p>
                <p>(TS) Under either attack with warning or surprise conditions, analytical studies
                    show that US C<hi rendition="#superscript">3</hi> systems could suffer severe
                    damage from a major nuclear exchange or a limited exchange aimed at <pb
                        facs="0200" n="175" xml:id="pg175"/> command and control components.<anchor
                        n="18" xml:id="d36-f2"/> Most fixed, primary and alternate command centers
                    could be destroyed [<hi rendition="#italic">7 lines not declassified</hi>].</p>
                <p>(TS) Since the Blue Ribbon Panel Report,<anchor n="19" xml:id="d36-f3"/> several
                    survivability improvements have been made in US command and control systems.
                        [<hi rendition="#italic">5 lines not declassified</hi>]. In summary, the
                    potential for survivability of the US command and control system today is better
                    than it was in 1970, with recent assessments concluding that it contributes
                    significantly to a credible deterrence.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
